title A refusal that braids three answers -- recovering the lap that died at its send
prompt the standing diffuser seat, fire rota; the round open stashed a whole unsent lap ninety seconds after it staged
rota lap 4511, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels: law and boundary. Deep-read foundations/20260823-204456_single-stranded.md, and it named this lap's finding before I had words for it. A part is simple when you can pull it out. The roster runner's refusal cannot be pulled apart: one message says the lock is held, infers that its holder is void, and prescribes a kill -- and the third strand rests on the second, so a wrong middle reaches the hand as one confident sentence.
think Opened on a tree whose git status was clean and whose stash list ran seventeen deep. stash@{0} was stamped 20260910-022111, ninety seconds before my own clock read, so it was made by MY round open rather than by an elder lap. Reading its diff stat found twenty-one files, 681 insertions -- a whole guard family, a paper, a roster row, a session log.
think Read the parked lap's own record before touching its code. It carried `status GREEN` with five witnesses named, written BEFORE the send exactly as the baton asks. That field is what made the recovery a verification rather than a guess: a lap killed mid-send and a lap that declined to send read identically without it.
think Applied rather than popped, so the stash survived a bad merge. Four files conflicted with peers landed since: tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_scan.sh, README.md, session-logs/CHAPTERS.md, session-logs/README.md. Resolved each by DERIVING rather than by choosing a side -- the 20260909 shelf holds 105 rows on disk, so both rosters take 105; the tutorial repair took the peer's landed form, which reads diff's own status and refuses 2 or more where the parked version tolerated more broadly.
think Regenerated README.md's metrics block AFTER git add rather than before, because readme_metrics_scan counts with git ls-files, which reads the INDEX -- a page written ahead of staging carries a number one short. It read 1911 witnesses over 1734 modules, so the parked lap's symlink fix holds against a tree that moved under it.
think Re-proved on metal rather than off the log: link_counted GREEN, readme_metrics GREEN, ascii_document GREEN, living_pin_near_bound GREEN. Four of the five the parked record named, each run here, at this HEAD, with the peers' commits in.
obs The finding is about an instrument rather than about the lap. The roster runner refused my cold open naming pid 1947712, said the process table cannot know whether a --detach pass is still here, called its verdict already fixed at tree_moved, and advised kill -TERM. Every clause was true except the framing: that pass was ninety seconds old, its transcript was growing line by line, and it was the parked lap's own hot pass. The TERM was correct to send and it did land -- yet re-running the runner thirty seconds later printed a BYTE-IDENTICAL refusal, so nothing in the instrument distinguishes a TERM pending from a TERM ignored, and the very next sentence offers SIGKILL, which that same sentence says leaves the lock behind.
obs Then I measured it rather than handing it forward. Stopping my own stale hot pass, I timed TERM to lock release by polling for the lock directory: 13 seconds, and 22 on a second reading later in the lap. That is the figure the refusal never prints, and it is long enough for a hand to re-run the runner and read the identical refusal a second time, which is exactly what happened to me an hour earlier. The mechanism stays unproven: the trap block reads `trap 'exit 143' TERM` and POSIX defers a trap until a foreground child returns, which would bound the delay by the slowest guard -- yet the slowest guard the transcript reports is exec_bit at 3,979 ms while the pass advances at roughly ten seconds per guard, so the reported figure is not the child's wall time. Observation measured, inference withheld.
obs The falsifier for the finding, plainly: if the runner's refusal printed the lock's age and whether a signal had already been delivered, a hand could tell a pending stop from an ignored one, and this finding would have nothing left to say. That is the repair I am proposing rather than a defect I am asserting.
obs What the Air reading adds: this is a braid rather than an error. Whether the lock is held, whether its holder is void, and whether a hand should signal it are three questions, and the refusal answers all three in one voice. %681 cured the middle strand by replacing an ancestry reading with the lock's own launch_head -- correct, and it left the braid. A reader who doubts the middle cannot pull it out.
obs Held the pin by tightening rather than raising, as Patchouli's row did last night. My account plus the recovery ran 41,520 bytes against a 40,960 bound; six passes of compression brought it to 40,957, and every sentence shed is held whole in the paper the account links or in the parked lap's own record. No checkpoint owed, because nothing was condensed that stands only here.
